  <Icon>k__BackingField: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 44f0b2c512093924f82fc83e489e9cac, type: 3}
  <AboutText>k__BackingField: '<b><size=+10>Overview</size></b>

    Golf Solitaire
    is a simple game and serves as the originator of the aptly named "Golf" genre
    of card games. It requires one standard 52-card deck with 35 cards dealt evenly
    across 7 columns.

    

    Golf requires the player to remove cards in sequence
    until there are no more left on the board. A card sequence can be any combination
    of cards, as long as the next card in sequence is one rank higher or lower than
    the previous one (A<->2<->3<->4...). Victory is achieved when all 35 cards have
    been removed from the board. Winning Golf requires a mixture of luck and skill.

    

    <b><size=+10>History</size></b>

    Golf
    Solitaire finds its origin under the name "One Foundation" in the 1905 book "Games
    of Patience" by Tarbart. This game''s rules are almost identical to the modern
    rules of Golf, with only minor variations having accumulated over the decades.
    The primary variant allows for "wrap around" of the cards, allowing players to
    place an Ace on a King and vice-versa, since in the original rules Ace and King
    were the ends of the sequence. Additionally, another harder variant was popular
    where nothing, not even a Queen, could be played on a King.

    

    The
    way that One Foundation became known as Golf is due to the game''s low win rate
    when using the traditional rules. Players began to score themselves based on
    how many cards remained when they ran out of moves, with a lower score meaning
    that you did better. Due to the actual game of Golf having similar scoring rules,
    One Foundation gradually became known as Golf.

    

    

    <b><size=+10>Today</size></b>

    Golf
    Solitaire, while less mainstream than games like Klondike or Spider, remains
    a popular card game. Its simple rules and numerous variants allow for players
    of all skill levels to enjoy it, and the Golf genre has spawned numerous card
    games which themselves have become popular.'
  <GuideObject>k__BackingField: {fileID: 6214578951917086794, guid: 630c4c1cb94f32943ab9dfa337c39652, type: 3}
